EXT. OPEN OCEAN - INFLATABLE DINGHY - DAY

A grey swell lifts the orange raft and lets it fall again, patient as a hand. The sky has no edges. Three men lie in the bottom of the boat like fish nobody wanted.

GUNNAR PEDERSEN, fifties, sunburnt to the colour of brick, tends a bailing cup. FELIX ODUYA, thirties, the youngest, stares at his own cracked palms. TARIQ MANSOOR, forties, the engineer, sits upright against the pontoon with the last water jug cradled in his lap.

GUNNAR
(hoarse)
Give him some.

TARIQ
He had his share.

GUNNAR
He is shaking.

TARIQ
He is cold. He is not thirsty.

Felix lifts his head. His lips are split.

FELIX
I can hear it. Listen. A motor.

Gunnar and Tariq both go still. Wind. Slap of water on rubber. Nothing.

TARIQ
You hear the sea.

FELIX
It's a motor.

He lurches to his knees, rocking the raft. Gunnar grabs the rope. Tariq clutches the jug. Felix stands, arms high, waving at the empty horizon, shouting a name nobody recognises.

GUNNAR
Sit, boy. Sit.

Felix collapses. The jug tilts. A mouthful of water spills across Tariq's knees. He stares at it, soaking into the cloth. Then, very slowly, he lifts the jug and offers it across the raft. His hand shakes. He is not looking at either of them.

TARIQ
(quietly)
Drink.
